Known failure: no-gained-errors — the formatter splits glued === and !== into '== =' / '!= =', adding unexpected_token (issue to file)
JavaScript-style strict operators === and !== are not SSL; the
invalid_operator_sequence rule reports them, and formatting must not
re-tokenize them into a different error.
-- input.ssl --
:PROCEDURE Demo;
:DECLARE bSame, bDiffer;
bSame := 1 === 1;
bDiffer := 1 !== 2;
:RETURN bSame .AND. bDiffer;
:ENDPROC;
-- format --
:PROCEDURE Demo;
	:DECLARE bSame, bDiffer;
	bSame := 1 == = 1;
	bDiffer := 1 != = 2;
	:RETURN bSame .AND. bDiffer;
:ENDPROC;
-- diagnostics --
3:12-3:15 error invalid_operator_sequence: SSL uses '==' for exact equality, not '==='. Use '==' instead
4:14-4:17 error invalid_operator_sequence: SSL uses '!=' or '<>' for inequality, not '!=='. Use '!=' instead
-- diagnostics [--strict --info] --
3:12-3:15 error invalid_operator_sequence: SSL uses '==' for exact equality, not '==='. Use '==' instead
4:14-4:17 error invalid_operator_sequence: SSL uses '!=' or '<>' for inequality, not '!=='. Use '!=' instead
